## C.5: Predictive Strategy — Markov-Chain-Driven Tick Allocation

### What To Build

The crown jewel: a TickStrategy that uses the MarkovPredictor to dynamically allocate tick rates based on learned transition probabilities. Screens the user is likely to switch to get ticked more frequently; unlikely screens get throttled.

### How It Works

On each frame, for each inactive screen:
1. Look up the cached divisor for this screen
2. Check if tick_count is a multiple of that divisor
3. Return Tick or Skip accordingly

Divisors are recomputed when the active screen changes (not every frame).

### Implementation

```rust
pub struct Predictive {
    predictor: MarkovPredictor<String>,
    allocation: TickAllocation,
    fallback_divisor: u64,  // used when predictor has zero confidence
    persistence_path: Option<PathBuf>,
    /// Cache: recomputed only when active screen changes.
    cached_divisors: HashMap<String, u64>,
    cached_for_screen: Option<String>,
}

impl Predictive {
    pub fn new(config: PredictiveConfig) -> Self;
    pub fn with_persisted_data(config: PredictiveConfig, data: TransitionCounter<String>) -> Self;
    pub fn predictor(&self) -> &MarkovPredictor<String>;

    fn refresh_cache(&mut self, active: &str) {
        if self.cached_for_screen.as_deref() != Some(active) {
            let predictions = self.predictor.predict(active);
            self.cached_divisors = predictions.iter()
                .map(|p| (p.screen.clone(), self.allocation.divisor_for(p.probability)))
                .collect();
            self.cached_for_screen = Some(active.to_string());
        }
    }
}

impl TickStrategy for Predictive {
    fn should_tick(&mut self, screen_id: &str, tick_count: u64, active: &str) -> TickDecision {
        let divisor = self.cached_divisors
            .get(screen_id)
            .copied()
            .unwrap_or(self.fallback_divisor);
        if tick_count.is_multiple_of(divisor) {
            TickDecision::Tick
        } else {
            TickDecision::Skip
        }
    }

    fn on_screen_transition(&mut self, from: &str, to: &str) {
        self.predictor.record_transition(from.to_string(), to.to_string());
        // Recompute divisors for new active screen
        self.refresh_cache(to);
    }

    fn maintenance_tick(&mut self, tick_count: u64) {
        if tick_count.is_multiple_of(100) {
            self.predictor.maybe_decay();
        }
        // Auto-save every 3000 ticks (~5 minutes at 10fps)
        if tick_count.is_multiple_of(3000) {
            if let Some(path) = &self.persistence_path {
                let _ = save_transitions(self.predictor.counter(), path);
            }
        }
    }

    fn shutdown(&mut self) {
        if let Some(path) = &self.persistence_path {
            let _ = save_transitions(self.predictor.counter(), path);
        }
    }

    fn name(&self) -> &str { "Predictive" }

    fn debug_stats(&self) -> Vec<(String, String)> {
        vec![
            ("strategy".into(), "Predictive".into()),
            ("total_transitions".into(), format!("{}", self.predictor.counter().total())),
            // ... more stats
        ]
    }
}
```

### Key Design Points

1. **Divisor caching**: Per-screen divisors are cached in a HashMap and only recomputed when `on_screen_transition()` fires (active screen changed). Cache hit rate is ~100% since screen changes are rare (user-initiated). This avoids running the prediction engine on every frame.

2. **C.5 owns TickAllocation**: The Predictive strategy holds a TickAllocation (B.2) instance and uses it to convert MarkovPredictor probabilities to divisors. B.1 only returns probabilities/confidence; C.5 does the probability→divisor conversion.

3. **shutdown() saves persistence**: Called by the runtime during clean Program teardown. Uses atomic write via E.1.

4. **maintenance_tick()**: Handles both periodic decay (every 100 ticks) and auto-save (every 3000 ticks ~= 5 min). These intervals are in PredictiveConfig.

### Cold-Start Behavior

When the predictor has low confidence (cold start), it blends predictions toward uniform, which means all screens get similar divisors close to fallback_divisor. This makes Predictive degrade gracefully to Uniform behavior.

### PredictiveConfig

